I've written the plan: two task files and an issue-responses file in session_plan/. The commit step didn't record anything because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. I didn't force-add it; the harness should read the files from disk.

Task 1: Fixes #1223 (my own pick). The hard-block layer treats a quoted mention like git commit -m "fix rm -rf / at $(date)" as a real command once a $(...) or backtick appears anywhere in it, and --yes can't override that. The gentler checking layer already treats the same text as a mention. The task:
• Post a before-table on #1223 before editing any code.
• Fix the step that deletes every quote character once a substitution appears. Command text inside $(...),  ...  and quote-split words like r"m" must stay refused.
• Run the old and new matchers once over a generated set of commands and list every case that changes, in both directions. That comparison is not kept as a test; the committed tests check expected results against the new matcher only.
• Run a deliberate-break check that turns the new tests red by name, then restore it.

Task 2: Fixes #1224 (backlog clean-up). There are 19 open self-filed issues; #1224 has no comments and the least work left. /spawn --parallel -o x.md ... reads the path and drops it without a word. My decision: refuse before anything runs and name -o, rather than invent a combined output file. The refusal also shows when --force is on the same line. One warning for whoever implements it: commands_spawn.rs is only 8 lines under its size limit, and going over makes cargo test fail, which reverts the whole task. So the new code goes in commands_spawn_args.rs. The /spawn docs get one sentence.

